Brain Tumor Detection using Convolutional Neural Network
Project Description
This project focuses on automating the diagnosis of brain tumors using deep learning techniques. The goal is to develop a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) model that can accurately detect and classify brain tumors from MRI images. This initiative aims to assist in the early and efficient diagnosis of brain tumors within real-world healthcare systems, underscoring the potential of AI in medical diagnostics.

Methodology
The project employs a comprehensive deep learning pipeline, including:

Data Sourcing and Preparation: The dataset is sourced from Kaggle. The images undergo preprocessing steps such as standardization and data augmentation to improve model robustness. The data is split into training, validation, and testing sets.

Model Architecture: A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is built using the Keras and TensorFlow frameworks. The model consists of convolutional, pooling, and dense layers designed for image classification.

Training and Evaluation: The model is trained on the prepared dataset and evaluated using standard metrics to assess its performance in identifying different types of brain tumors, which include pituitary, glioma, meningioma, and no tumor.

Installation and Usage
To run this project, you need to have Python and a Jupyter Notebook environment installed.

Clone the Repository

git clone https://github.com/your-username/your-repository.git
cd your-repository
Install Dependencies
The project relies on several key Python libraries. You can install them using pip:

pip install pandas numpy tensorflow keras scikit-learn matplotlib seaborn kagglehub
You will also need to set up your Kaggle API credentials to download the dataset.

Download the Dataset
The project uses the "masoudnickparvar/brain-tumor-mri-dataset" from Kaggle. The included Jupyter Notebook (Braintumor.ipynb) contains the code to automatically download this dataset.

Run the Notebook
Open the Braintumor.ipynb notebook and run the cells sequentially to see the full data processing, model training, and evaluation pipeline.

Results
The project's performance is detailed in the Machine Learning - Final Project1.docx document, which includes a comprehensive analysis of the model's accuracy, loss, and other relevant metrics on the validation and test datasets.
